a special session of the state  legislature is officially underway in  carson city the governor called state  lawmakers to go over redistricting maps  that were revealed earlier this week it  is done every 10 years joe mohler joins  us in the studio with what exactly  happened joe o'brien lawmakers gathered  for a few hours this afternoon and  created committees that will discuss the  proposed new maps they will have to  create new boundaries with roughly the  same number of people in each one  every 10 years the population is counted  in the census nevada added 400 thousand  people over the past decade that means  political districts need to be redrawn  to accommodate population changes that's  what this redistricting is going to do  it's setting the rules unlv political  expert dan lee says the state has seen a  lot of changes the matter has been on  this trend over the past really 20 years  of becoming more racially diverse  lawmakers will look at boundaries for  state senate and assembly congressional  districts and board of regents lee  explained how this could impact the  congressional districts because  democrats are you know they currently  have a three to one advantage and  they're going to try to  shore up that advantage so they're going  to try to redraw the districts  that probably makes the third district  for instance a little bit safer  democrats control the legislature and  governor's office republicans would have  to file legal challenges to fight any  changes currently we have  38 percent of our people are  non-partisans  34 percent are democrats and about 30  percent are republicans state assembly  woman robin titus says she isn't happy  with the proposed maps these maps that  the democrats have submitted  all it does is secure that the democrats  will stay in a super majority for the  next decade subcommittees will have to  do three readings of the maps on three  different days before a vote can happen  we did reach out to democrat officials  for comment but we did not hear back  today lawmakers will reconvene tomorrow  morning reporting in the studio joe  muller live local now
 

